        "role": "comparison",
        "intent": "Connect the mechanism to a benefit hypothesis without fabricating evidence.",
        "layout": {
          "composition": "Before-and-proposal journey rows with unnumbered step boxes. Highlight repeated sign-in boxes qualitatively; no chart, percentages, stopwatch or numeric score."
        },
        "visual_elements": {
          "main_visual": "Before-and-proposal journey rows with unnumbered step boxes. Highlight repeated sign-in boxes qualitatively; no chart, percentages, stopwatch or numeric score."
        },
        "required_images": [],
        "notes": "The possible benefit comes from the steps a user would no longer need to repeat. A recognized session could reduce another sign-in. A common account could also reduce the need to remember which account applies to which service.\n\nWe cannot say how much time this would save, or how often the opportunity occurs, without understanding real journeys. For this discussion, the claim is qualitative: the proposal could remove repeated account interactions. We next need to consider whether the engineering responsibilities remain clear."
      },
      {
        "number": 5,
        "title": "Authentication and access are different",
        "language": "English",
        "key_points": [
          "Authentication: who is signing in",
          "Authorization: what they may access",
          "Account linking must preserve the distinction"
        ],
        "role": "concept explanation",
        "intent": "Explain the distinction so account recognition does not overclaim permissions.",
        "layout": {
          "composition": "Two panels: identity icon at left, service-specific access gates at right; clean labelled divider."
        },
        "visual_elements": {
          "main_visual": "Two panels: identity icon at left, service-specific access gates at right; clean labelled divider."
        },
        "required_images": [],
        "notes": "A shared account helps answer who is signing in. It does not, by itself, decide what that person may do in each service. We still need a clear place for each access decision and a way to explain it to users and support teams.\n\nWhen accounts are connected or migrated, that distinction matters. We should not assume that recognizing a person automatically transfers every existing permission. The next conversation is about the ownership and failure cases behind this model."
      },
      {
        "number": 6,
        "title": "The trade-offs to examine",
        "language": "English",
        "key_points": [
          "Account ownership and recovery",
          "Cross-service privacy and permissions",
          "Availability and migration"
        ],
        "role": "comparison",
        "intent": "Provide a compact map of unresolved concerns.",
        "layout": {
          "composition": "Three balanced concern cards, each with a simple line icon; no severity ranking."
        },
        "visual_elements": {
          "main_visual": "Three balanced concern cards, each with a simple line icon; no severity ranking."
        },
        "required_images": [],
        "notes": "There are three groups of trade-offs I would bring into the design discussion. First, ownership: who manages the account and helps a user recover it? Second, boundaries: what information or permissions cross a service boundary, and what stays local? Third, operation: how a shared dependency and a transition from existing accounts affect availability and rollout.\n\nThese are investigation areas, not evidence that a particular design is unsafe. The proposal is useful only if we can make these responsibilities understandable and workable."
      },
      {
        "number": 7,
        "title": "Questions before a rollout",
        "language": "English",
        "key_points": [
          "Which services and user groups are in scope?",
          "Who owns support and account recovery?",
          "How will users move from existing accounts?"
        ],
        "role": "workshop questions",
        "intent": "Make the engineering discussion concrete while leaving unprovided answers open.",
        "layout": {
          "composition": "Three investigation lanes for scope, ownership and transition; questions remain unanswered."
        },
        "visual_elements": {
          "main_visual": "Three investigation lanes for scope, ownership and transition; questions remain unanswered."
        },
        "required_images": [],
        "notes": "Before we discuss a rollout, I would make the scope explicit. Which services participate first, and which user journeys do we want to understand? Then I would establish who owns account support and recovery. Finally, I would map how existing users and accounts enter the model.\n\nThose questions help us turn a broad shared login idea into a proposal we can examine. They also identify the situations in which we could later observe whether repeated account steps disappear."
      },
      {
        "number": 8,
        "title": "What this proposal establishes",
        "language": "English",
        "key_points": [
          "A mechanism for removing repeated access steps",
          "A benefit hypothesis that remains unmeasured",
          "Open questions about ownership, access and rollout"
        ],
        "role": "summary",
        "intent": "End with shared understanding, matching the confirmed purpose.",
        "layout": {
          "composition": "Three takeaway blocks with a small shared-account diagram echo; no call to approve deployment."
        },
        "visual_elements": {
          "main_visual": "Three takeaway blocks with a small shared-account diagram echo; no call to approve deployment."
        },
        "required_images": [],
        "notes": "The proposal gives us a mechanism to discuss: connected services could recognize one account and make some repeated sign-in steps unnecessary. It gives us a possible user benefit, but it does not establish the size of that benefit.\n\nThe engineering work is to make ownership, access decisions and rollout clear enough that we can assess the model. That is the understanding I want us to share today, with the time-saving claim still treated as a hypothesis."
      }
    ]
  },
